00:03:30,930 --> 00:03:33,570
the bootstrap exercise,

78
00:03:33,570 --> 00:03:34,990
because, as you can already see,

79
00:03:34,990 --> 00:03:36,370
we take the other seven and

80
00:03:36,370 --> 00:03:37,350
actually suspend them.

81
00:03:37,350 --> 00:03:40,750
So what we do later, in fact,

82
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:43,210
is implement multitasking on a single core.

83
00:03:43,210 --> 00:03:44,750
So on one core,

84
00:03:44,750 --> 00:03:45,890
on one hart,

85
00:03:45,890 --> 00:03:47,530
how do we make multiple tasks

86
00:03:47,530 --> 00:03:48,590
run at the same time?

87
00:03:48,590 --> 00:03:50,750
Well, what we actually adopt is this

88
00:03:50,750 --> 00:03:52,090
time-sharing approach,

89
00:03:52,090 --> 00:03:56,510
that is, letting these two tasks take turns executing,

90
00:03:56,510 --> 00:03:59,230
letting the two tasks take turns executing,

91
00:03:59,230 --> 00:04:02,650
taking turns using this hart to achieve it.

92
00:04:02,650 --> 00:04:04,470
So each one,

93
00:04:04,470 --> 00:04:06,130
we know that once a hart is running,

94
00:04:06,130 --> 00:04:07,550
during its execution,

95
00:04:07,550 --> 00:04:11,250
its execution state of the hart is really reflected

96
00:04:11,250 --> 00:04:12,250
basically in

97
00:04:12,250 --> 00:04:16,270
the changes of those 32 registers.

98
00:04:16,270 --> 00:04:19,230
So if we want two tasks

99
00:04:19,230 --> 00:04:20,350
to be able to execute at the same time,

100
00:04:20,350 --> 00:04:23,670
then we will inevitably need to, at the software level,

101
00:04:23,670 --> 00:04:26,250
For each of these tasks,

102
00:04:26,250 --> 00:04:28,810
the values of the registers it is currently using

103
00:04:28,810 --> 00:04:30,050
actually need to be backed up.

104
00:04:30,050 --> 00:04:31,470
For example, if we want to let

105
00:04:31,470 --> 00:04:32,990
the task on the left run,

106
00:04:32,990 --> 00:04:35,170
we need to take the register information

107
00:04:35,170 --> 00:04:36,270
of the task on the right,

108
00:04:36,270 --> 00:04:38,030
back it up, and save it.

109
00:04:38,030 --> 00:04:40,210
Then, when we put

110
00:04:40,210 --> 00:04:43,310
the instruction stream on the right,

111
00:04:43,310 --> 00:04:44,470
onto the CPU,

112
00:04:44,470 --> 00:04:45,410
load it up and run it,

113
00:04:45,410 --> 00:04:47,070
we just need to take the information above

114
00:04:47,070 --> 00:04:49,270
and restore it back up there, and that's it.

115
00:04:49,270 --> 00:04:50,570
So really, it's about doing this

116
00:04:50,570 --> 00:04:52,450
kind of switching,

117
00:04:52,450 --> 00:04:53,690
this kind of switch.

118
00:04:53,690 --> 00:04:55,850
Now, in the process of this switching,

119
00:04:55,850 --> 00:04:56,570
these instructions,

120
00:04:56,570 --> 00:04:57,930
these registers,

121
00:04:57,930 --> 00:05:00,110
This is actually what we call

122
00:05:00,110 --> 00:05:02,150
the concept of context.

123
00:05:02,150 --> 00:05:03,670
So if we say

124
00:05:03,670 --> 00:05:05,690
we want to run multiple tasks on one core,

125
00:05:05,690 --> 00:05:07,150
say we have two tasks,

126
00:05:07,150 --> 00:05:08,650
then the context of both tasks

127
00:05:08,650 --> 00:05:09,670
needs to be maintained by us,

128
00:05:09,670 --> 00:05:11,110
and we keep switching

129
00:05:11,110 --> 00:05:12,970
which task is running on the CPU.

130
00:05:12,970 --> 00:05:14,910
We need to restore this task's

131
00:05:14,910 --> 00:05:17,690
context onto the CPU to run,

132
00:05:17,690 --> 00:05:19,210
while at the same time saving

133
00:05:19,210 --> 00:05:21,150
the context of the other task

134
00:05:21,150 --> 00:05:22,170
away, and then do the switch.

135
00:05:22,170 --> 00:05:24,250
Through this context switching,

136
00:05:24,250 --> 00:05:26,030
we actually achieve the effect of multiple tasks

137
00:05:26,030 --> 00:05:29,970
multiplexing a single CPU,

138
00:05:29,970 --> 00:05:31,050
a single hart.

139
00:05:31,050 --> 00:05:33,250
This is actually equivalent to what we call

140
00:05:33,250 --> 00:05:34,270
the concept of a thread.

141
00:05:34,270 --> 00:05:36,350
Of course, we call them tasks here.

142
00:05:36,350 --> 00:05:37,210
That's a more general term.

143
00:05:37,210 --> 00:05:40,250
So what I'm telling you here is this:

144
00:05:40,250 --> 00:05:41,070
the so-called task,

145
00:05:41,070 --> 00:05:41,990
actually corresponds to

146
00:05:41,990 --> 00:05:44,670
the current values of these registers.

147
00:05:44,670 --> 00:05:47,030
So when we maintain them,

148
00:05:47,030 --> 00:05:48,310
when we save and restore them,

149
00:05:48,310 --> 00:05:49,470
we can actually, in memory,

150
00:05:49,470 --> 00:05:50,570
define a struct like this.

151
00:05:50,570 --> 00:05:53,850
We do have this struct in our code.

152
00:05:53,850 --> 00:05:56,150
You can look at our... this...

153
00:05:56,150 --> 00:05:59,150
Since today this slide corresponds to

154
00:05:59,150 --> 00:06:03,170
our 03 context switch in Shanghai.

