00:07:23,550 --> 00:07:24,250
as we learned earlier,

179
00:07:24,250 --> 00:07:25,210
is actually

180
00:07:25,210 --> 00:07:27,030
a register whose value never changes.

181
00:07:27,030 --> 00:07:27,990
So we don't need to

182
00:07:27,990 --> 00:07:29,470
maintain it.

183
00:07:29,470 --> 00:07:30,090
As for the other values,

184
00:07:30,090 --> 00:07:30,990
we do need to maintain them.

185
00:07:30,990 --> 00:07:33,640
Good.

186
00:07:33,640 --> 00:07:35,540
This is what's known as the concept of context.

187
00:07:35,540 --> 00:07:39,490
Now, with this concept in place,

188
00:07:39,490 --> 00:07:40,630
let's take a look at

189
00:07:40,630 --> 00:07:44,130
the so-called cooperative multitasking design

190
00:07:44,130 --> 00:07:45,430
and how to implement it.

191
00:07:45,430 --> 00:07:48,120
So here,

192
00:07:48,120 --> 00:07:48,760
we should mention one thing,

193
00:07:48,760 --> 00:07:49,820
which is that

194
00:07:49,820 --> 00:07:52,120
when it comes to multitasking,

195
00:07:52,120 --> 00:07:53,200
we have

196
00:07:53,200 --> 00:07:55,400
in principle,

197
00:07:55,400 --> 00:07:56,460
we divide it into two types:

198
00:07:56,460 --> 00:07:58,280
one is called cooperative multitasking,

199
00:07:58,280 --> 00:08:00,200
and the other is called preemptive multitasking.

200
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:02,020
What we ultimately want to implement is a

201
00:08:02,020 --> 00:08:03,160
Preemptive multitasking

202
00:08:03,160 --> 00:08:04,340
But before that,

203
00:08:04,340 --> 00:08:05,380
before we do that,

204
00:08:05,380 --> 00:08:06,080
let's first take a look at

205
00:08:06,080 --> 00:08:07,640
how to implement a cooperative multitasking,

206
00:08:07,640 --> 00:08:08,960
how do we implement it?

207
00:08:08,960 --> 00:08:10,380
So, what is

208
00:08:10,380 --> 00:08:11,800
cooperative multitasking?

209
00:08:11,800 --> 00:08:13,160
In English, it's called

210
00:08:13,160 --> 00:08:15,380
Cooperative Multitasking.

211
00:08:15,380 --> 00:08:17,240
So what does it mean?

212
00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:18,380
Well, imagine this:

213
00:08:18,380 --> 00:08:20,160
suppose we now

214
00:08:20,160 --> 00:08:21,540
have two tasks,

215
00:08:21,540 --> 00:08:23,220
the two tasks

216
00:08:23,220 --> 00:08:24,380
are like two people,

217
00:08:24,380 --> 00:08:29,860
and they have to share one HUD,

218
00:08:29,860 --> 00:08:31,620
one CPU.

219
00:08:31,620 --> 00:08:34,520
So what does cooperation mean?

220
00:08:34,520 --> 00:08:36,240
Well, say at the beginning,

221
00:08:36,240 --> 00:08:38,300
It is this person running on the CPU.

222
00:08:38,300 --> 00:08:40,500
Then after it runs for a while,

223
00:08:40,500 --> 00:08:42,200
it needs to be able to actively

224
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:43,860
yield the CPU.

225
00:08:43,860 --> 00:08:45,080
After yielding it,

226
00:08:45,080 --> 00:08:46,420
it essentially hands it over to the other one,

227
00:08:46,420 --> 00:08:48,000
and then it comes in and runs.

228
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:49,260
After it runs for a while,

229
00:08:49,260 --> 00:08:51,220
it yields the CPU again,

230
00:08:51,220 --> 00:08:51,940
handing it over to the other one,

231
00:08:51,940 --> 00:08:52,820
and then that one comes in and runs.

232
00:08:52,820 --> 00:08:54,060
So it is essentially two people

233
00:08:54,060 --> 00:08:57,180
actively giving this up,

234
00:08:57,180 --> 00:08:59,180
actively giving up the CPU,

235
00:08:59,180 --> 00:09:01,880
so that other tasks have a chance to execute.

236
00:09:01,880 --> 00:09:05,160
Now, this way of working

237
00:09:05,160 --> 00:09:08,080
is called cooperative multitasking.

238
00:09:08,080 --> 00:09:09,580
Cooperative multitasking.

239
00:09:09,580 --> 00:09:12,340
In fact, we probably don't encounter it much these days,

240
00:09:12,340 --> 00:09:14,300
but in early operating systems,

241
00:09:14,300 --> 00:09:16,760
It really is a cooperative approach

242
00:09:16,760 --> 00:09:17,800
to implementing multitasking.

243
00:09:17,800 --> 00:09:20,240
For example, early systems like DOS,

244
00:09:20,240 --> 00:09:21,520
these older operating systems,

245
00:09:21,520 --> 00:09:22,680
they actually worked this way.

246
00:09:22,680 --> 00:09:23,940
Now, with this approach,

247
00:09:23,940 --> 00:09:25,160
you naturally get

248
00:09:25,160 --> 00:09:26,080
the effect where two tasks

249
00:09:26,080 --> 00:09:28,960
take turns

250
00:09:28,960 --> 00:09:31,260
running on the CPU.

251
00:09:31,260 --> 00:09:32,060
But as you know,

252
00:09:32,060 --> 00:09:32,780
this approach

253
00:09:32,780 --> 00:09:35,540
also has a very obvious problem,

254
00:09:35,540 --> 00:09:36,080
namely,

255
00:09:36,080 --> 00:09:37,960
it requires that,

256
00:09:37,960 --> 00:09:39,720
when we write these tasks,

257
00:09:39,720 --> 00:09:40,840
we know a task,

258
00:09:40,840 --> 00:09:41,380
as we mentioned earlier,

259
00:09:41,380 --> 00:09:42,400
is really just a function,

260
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:42,880
right?

261
00:09:42,880 --> 00:09:44,560
Now these functions

262
00:09:44,560 --> 00:09:46,820
in the end, are still written by us.

263
00:09:46,820 --> 00:09:48,380
So if you need to

264
00:09:48,380 --> 00:09:50,020
actively give up the CPU,

265
00:09:50,020 --> 00:09:51,880
that means in this code,

266
00:09:51,880 --> 00:09:53,480
you have to call some special functions

267
00:09:53,480 --> 00:09:54,720
to release the CPU,

268
00:09:54,720 --> 00:09:57,640
that is, to voluntarily give it up.

269
00:09:57,640 --> 00:09:58,880
But this thing,

270
00:09:58,880 --> 00:10:00,260
if the programmer

271
00:10:00,260 --> 00:10:01,220
is not careful when writing it,

272
00:10:01,220 --> 00:10:02,000
for example, if he just

273
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,080
forgets to do this,

274
00:10:04,080 --> 00:10:05,380
and he ends up in an infinite loop,

275
00:10:05,380 --> 00:10:05,780
think about it,

276
00:10:05,780 --> 00:10:07,200
if his code is badly written

277
00:10:07,200 --> 00:10:07,880
and gets stuck in an infinite loop,

278
00:10:07,880 --> 00:10:08,620
he never gets the chance

279
00:10:08,620 --> 00:10:09,700
to release the CPU,

280
00:10:09,700 --> 00:10:10,740
then the other tasks

281
00:10:10,740 --> 00:10:11,640
there's no way to execute

282
00:10:11,640 --> 00:10:13,620
so with this approach,

283
00:10:13,620 --> 00:10:15,620
it's very easy for our operating system

284
00:10:15,620 --> 00:10:17,220
in fact, it's very easy to

285
00:10:17,220 --> 00:10:18,580
be written badly by tasks

286
00:10:18,580 --> 00:10:19,520
and get deadlocked by them

287
00:10:19,520 --> 00:10:21,860
so this cooperative multitasking

288
00:10:21,860 --> 00:10:23,480
does have certain problems

289
00:10:23,480 --> 00:10:26,140
then later we evolved into,

290
00:10:26,140 --> 00:10:27,840
that is, using preemptive multitasking

291
00:10:27,840 --> 00:10:29,260
now with preemptive multitasking,

292
00:10:29,260 --> 00:10:30,080
the idea is basically this:

293
00:10:30,080 --> 00:10:33,160
we don't want to let the current task

294
00:10:33,160 --> 00:10:34,520
do this by itself

295
00:10:34,520 --> 00:10:35,360
instead, the operating system

296
00:10:35,360 --> 00:10:38,200
decides the scheduling of tasks

297
00:10:38,200 --> 00:10:39,360
it can

298
00:10:39,360 --> 00:10:41,620
our operating system can actively take away

299
00:10:41,620 --> 00:10:43,360
the current task's use of the processor

300
00:10:43,360 --> 00:10:45,580
and then give the processor to other tasks

301
00:10:45,580 --> 00:10:46,020
In this case,

302
00:10:46,020 --> 00:10:46,920
you can imagine,

303
00:10:46,920 --> 00:10:48,920
that is, even if your task

304
00:10:48,920 --> 00:10:49,860
falls into an infinite loop,

305
00:10:49,860 --> 00:10:51,260
it actually won't affect

306
00:10:51,260 --> 00:10:52,100
the entire system.

307
00:10:52,100 --> 00:10:54,400
Now, as for how to preempt it,

308
00:10:54,400 --> 00:10:56,060
we'll cover that later on.

309
00:10:56,060 --> 00:10:57,180
For now,

310
00:10:57,180 --> 00:10:57,900
let's first take a look at,

311
00:10:57,900 --> 00:10:58,380
that is,

312
00:10:58,380 --> 00:10:59,360
how simple

313
00:10:59,360 --> 00:11:01,440
cooperative multitasking is implemented.

314
00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:02,940
In this chapter,

315
00:11:02,940 --> 00:11:04,540
what we're implementing is

316
00:11:04,540 --> 00:11:08,570
cooperative multitasking.

317
00:11:08,570 --> 00:11:09,050
Right.

318
00:11:09,050 --> 00:11:10,750
So we'll

319
00:11:10,750 --> 00:11:12,270
explain it to you with a diagram,

320
00:11:12,270 --> 00:11:12,830
that is, this one here.

321
00:11:12,830 --> 00:11:15,310
One way to implement cooperative multitasking,

322
00:11:15,310 --> 00:11:15,670
that is,

323
00:11:15,670 --> 00:11:16,790
of course, there are many ways to implement it.

324
00:11:16,790 --> 00:11:17,790
Here, we'll

325
00:11:17,790 --> 00:11:20,250
take the approach used in our code

326
00:11:20,250 --> 00:11:21,090
and walk you through it.

327
00:11:21,090 --> 00:11:21,990
All right,

328
00:11:21,990 --> 00:11:22,230
so,

329
00:11:22,230 --> 00:11:23,230
over here,

330
00:11:23,230 --> 00:11:24,550
you can see there are two tasks.

331
00:11:24,550 --> 00:11:25,310
One is task A,

332
00:11:25,310 --> 00:11:25,950
and the other is task B.

333
00:11:25,950 --> 00:11:26,290
Right?

334
00:11:26,290 --> 00:11:29,270
Now, with these task A and task B,

335
00:11:29,270 --> 00:11:32,110
as we know, a task is

336
00:11:32,110 --> 00:11:33,110
really just a function.

337
00:11:33,110 --> 00:11:34,370
And a function,

338
00:11:34,370 --> 00:11:35,990
whether or not it calls other functions inside,

339
00:11:35,990 --> 00:11:36,310
as you know,

340
00:11:36,310 --> 00:11:38,950
you can think of it as simply a sequence of instructions.

341
00:11:38,950 --> 00:11:40,470
It's just a sequence of instructions.

342
00:11:40,470 --> 00:11:41,970
So look at task A here.

343
00:11:41,970 --> 00:11:43,450
The instructions go from

344
00:11:43,450 --> 00:11:44,370
for example,

345
00:11:44,370 --> 00:11:46,350
I, I plus 1, and so on,

346
00:11:46,350 --> 00:11:47,150
all the way to I plus M.

347
00:11:47,150 --> 00:11:48,050
That's one sequence of instructions.

348
00:11:48,050 --> 00:11:49,550
Then for task B,

349
00:11:49,550 --> 00:11:51,130
it starts from J, J plus 1,

350
00:11:51,130 --> 00:11:52,090
all the way to J plus N.

351
00:11:52,090 --> 00:11:53,130
That's also a sequence of instructions,

352
00:11:53,130 --> 00:11:53,910
right?

353
00:11:53,910 --> 00:11:55,650
So this is the first concept.

354
00:11:55,650 --> 00:11:56,930
Now the second one,

355
00:11:56,930 --> 00:11:58,190
since we said

356
00:11:58,190 --> 00:11:59,810
this is cooperative multitasking,

357
00:11:59,810 --> 00:12:01,790
that means we have to, within the task,

358
00:12:01,790 --> 00:12:02,970
insert some instructions ourselves,

359
00:12:02,970 --> 00:12:04,210
voluntarily giving up the CPU.

360
00:12:04,210 --> 00:12:06,430
So here, in the red part,

361
00:12:06,430 --> 00:12:08,530
So this call to switch2,

362
00:12:08,530 --> 00:12:10,590
is essentially this effect.

363
00:12:10,590 --> 00:12:12,670
Of course,

364
00:12:12,670 --> 00:12:14,730
the process of voluntarily giving up

365
00:12:14,730 --> 00:12:16,510
we summarize it as one

366
00:12:16,510 --> 00:12:19,030
function, switch2.

367
00:12:19,030 --> 00:12:21,430
So that's equivalent to saying

368
00:12:21,430 --> 00:12:22,070
this function,

369
00:12:22,070 --> 00:12:23,090
this task A,

370
00:12:23,090 --> 00:12:24,530
as soon as it calls switch2,

371
00:12:24,530 --> 00:12:26,870
we can imagine that

372
00:12:26,870 --> 00:12:29,690
it yields the CPU.

373
00:12:29,690 --> 00:12:30,890
Then at that point,

374
00:12:30,890 --> 00:12:32,130
we can take another task

375
00:12:32,130 --> 00:12:33,250
and load it in.

376
00:12:33,250 --> 00:12:35,630
So what exactly does switch2

377
00:12:35,630 --> 00:12:36,810
do in there?

378
00:12:36,810 --> 00:12:38,890
Actually, based on our earlier introduction,

379
00:12:38,890 --> 00:12:40,110
we can know that

380
00:12:40,110 --> 00:12:42,090
all it really does in there is one thing:

381
00:12:42,090 --> 00:12:46,050
That is to say, since it's going to do a switch,

382
00:12:46,050 --> 00:12:46,910
then it needs to

383
00:12:46,910 --> 00:12:49,350
not only switch this

384
00:12:49,350 --> 00:12:51,230
and load this task in,

385
00:12:51,230 --> 00:12:52,110
or take it out,

386
00:12:52,110 --> 00:12:54,230
there's actually one more main job it has to do,

387
00:12:54,230 --> 00:12:57,410
which is to save and restore this context.

388
00:12:57,410 --> 00:12:59,890
So you can see what it does,

389
00:12:59,890 --> 00:13:01,730
the first step is to save

390
00:13:01,730 --> 00:13:05,250
the context of the previous task,

391
00:13:05,250 --> 00:13:06,810
and then,

392
00:13:06,810 --> 00:13:08,330
at this point,

393
00:13:08,330 --> 00:13:09,230
it performs a switch,

394
00:13:09,230 --> 00:13:10,530
switching the context,

395
00:13:10,530 --> 00:13:11,690
which means switching the task.

396
00:13:11,690 --> 00:13:13,310
Then, when it switches,

397
00:13:13,310 --> 00:13:14,530
it still has to do

398
00:13:14,530 --> 00:13:15,190
one more thing afterwards,

399
00:13:15,190 --> 00:13:17,030
which is to take the new task's

400
00:13:17,030 --> 00:13:20,510
context and load it in.

401
00:13:20,510 --> 00:13:23,650
So actually, its main job boils down to these few steps.

402
00:13:23,650 --> 00:13:27,210
Now over here,

403
00:13:27,210 --> 00:13:28,570
look at the return of this function,

404
00:13:28,570 --> 00:13:29,790
the last return.

405
00:13:29,790 --> 00:13:31,970
Once this return is executed,

406
00:13:31,970 --> 00:13:33,090
we can achieve it so that

407
00:13:33,090 --> 00:13:36,170
a new task down below starts running.

408
00:13:36,170 --> 00:13:38,110
As for how that process works,

409
00:13:38,110 --> 00:13:38,910
how is it actually implemented?

410
00:13:38,910 --> 00:13:41,370
Let's look at the next diagram.

411
00:13:41,370 --> 00:13:45,010
So we know that right now these two tasks,

412
00:13:45,010 --> 00:13:46,350
assuming in this model of ours,

413
00:13:46,350 --> 00:13:48,350
there is only one CPU for the task,

414
00:13:48,350 --> 00:13:49,590
just one CPU.

415
00:13:49,590 --> 00:13:52,570
Now inside this CPU,

416
00:13:52,570 --> 00:13:54,490
we know that this CPU,

417
00:13:54,490 --> 00:13:55,790
has quite a few registers in it,

418
00:13:55,790 --> 00:13:56,570
right?

419
00:13:56,570 --> 00:13:58,310
There are general-purpose registers,

420
00:13:58,310 --> 00:14:00,470
and there are also some CSRs.

421
00:14:00,470 --> 00:14:02,550
that is, the control status register.

422
00:14:02,550 --> 00:14:05,150
Now, in here,

423
00:14:05,150 --> 00:14:07,550
there are two registers we mainly care about.

424
00:14:07,550 --> 00:14:09,110
The first register,

425
00:14:09,110 --> 00:14:12,330
is this register, ra.

426
00:14:12,330 --> 00:14:13,430
Because this thing

427
00:14:13,430 --> 00:14:15,330
is related to our ret and call instructions.

428
00:14:15,330 --> 00:14:16,970
Think back to

429
00:14:16,970 --> 00:14:18,090
when we talked about

430
00:14:18,090 --> 00:14:19,150
assembly instructions earlier,

431
00:14:19,150 --> 00:14:19,510
right here.

432
00:14:19,510 --> 00:14:21,530
All of these concepts will be used here.

433
00:14:21,530 --> 00:14:24,950
ra, as we know, is used to store the return address.

434
00:14:24,950 --> 00:14:25,770
So,

435
00:14:25,770 --> 00:14:28,650
there is also one more register,

436
00:14:28,650 --> 00:14:29,950
called mscratch, a register.

437
00:14:29,950 --> 00:14:30,810
This register,

438
00:14:30,810 --> 00:14:34,430
it is actually a register under privilege mode,

439
00:14:34,430 --> 00:14:37,650
a privileged register under Machine mode.

440
00:14:37,650 --> 00:14:39,210
We use this register,

441
00:14:39,210 --> 00:14:40,090
What is it used for?

442
00:14:40,090 --> 00:14:42,410
We now use it like a

443
00:14:42,410 --> 00:14:43,370
as a pointer,

444
00:14:43,370 --> 00:14:45,490
used to point to our

445
00:14:45,490 --> 00:14:49,470
which context is currently being handled

446
00:14:49,470 --> 00:14:51,190
So for this purpose,

447
00:14:51,190 --> 00:14:53,310
we actually need to give every task

448
00:14:53,310 --> 00:14:55,290
its own allocated context space.

449
00:14:55,290 --> 00:14:56,590
So these two things,

450
00:14:56,590 --> 00:14:58,950
you can think of context A and context B,

451
00:14:58,950 --> 00:15:01,070
as actually being located

452
00:15:01,070 --> 00:15:03,290
in two blocks of space in memory.

453
00:15:03,290 --> 00:15:04,090
Inside here,

454
00:15:04,090 --> 00:15:06,610
what is stored is this task's

455
00:15:06,610 --> 00:15:10,710
own general-purpose register contents.

456
00:15:10,710 --> 00:15:11,350
This actually corresponds to

457
00:15:11,350 --> 00:15:13,510
what we just introduced to you,

458
00:15:13,510 --> 00:15:17,110
which is really just a struct block like this,

459
00:15:17,110 --> 00:15:18,710
the context struct,

460
00:15:18,710 --> 00:15:21,610
which we define in memory.

461
00:15:21,610 --> 00:15:22,590
one for each task.

462
00:15:22,590 --> 00:15:24,650
And then we use this,

463
00:15:24,650 --> 00:15:26,850
which basically means we'll use

464
00:15:26,850 --> 00:15:29,090
this mscratch

465
00:15:29,090 --> 00:15:30,850
register,

466
00:15:30,850 --> 00:15:31,950
sort of like a pointer,

467
00:15:31,950 --> 00:15:33,930
pointing to it at one moment,

468
00:15:33,930 --> 00:15:35,770
and then to the other one at the next.

469
00:15:35,770 --> 00:15:38,290
That's how we implement the context switch.

470
00:15:38,290 --> 00:15:40,670
Switching context is basically just

471
00:15:40,670 --> 00:15:41,690
doing this.

472
00:15:41,690 --> 00:15:47,030
Let's take a look at how this process runs.

473
00:15:47,030 --> 00:15:48,730
Let me walk you through it.

474
00:15:48,730 --> 00:15:52,970
First, of course, the first step is to do some initialization.

475
00:15:52,970 --> 00:15:54,130
Now, the initialization work,

476
00:15:54,130 --> 00:15:59,190
we essentially need to initialize some of the contents in these registers.

477
00:15:59,190 --> 00:16:00,270
Initialize them.

478
00:16:00,270 --> 00:16:03,110
So at the very beginning,

479
00:16:03,110 --> 00:16:07,350
since we have two RAs, the addresses,

480
00:16:07,350 --> 00:16:12,590
we initialize both of them to the address of the first instruction of our task.

481
00:16:12,590 --> 00:16:15,970
On the left is i.

482
00:16:15,970 --> 00:16:17,650
On the right is j.

483
00:16:17,650 --> 00:16:19,510
This is the initialization.

484
00:16:19,510 --> 00:16:21,430
After the initialization is done,

485
00:16:21,430 --> 00:16:24,230
we then initialize this mscratch register

486
00:16:24,230 --> 00:16:25,050
as well.

487
00:16:25,050 --> 00:16:26,110
Its initialization, at the very beginning,

488
00:16:26,110 --> 00:16:27,070
let's assume the first one,

489
00:16:27,070 --> 00:16:29,170
the task loaded is task a,

490
00:16:29,170 --> 00:16:31,470
then we make this register,

491
00:16:31,470 --> 00:16:33,310
point to this,

492
00:16:33,310 --> 00:16:35,190
the context on the left.

493
00:16:35,190 --> 00:16:37,030
The left points to this,

494
00:16:37,030 --> 00:16:38,830
the address of this context on the left.

495
00:16:38,830 --> 00:16:39,730
It holds an address inside.

496
00:16:39,730 --> 00:16:42,090
All right, that's the initialization.

497
00:16:42,090 --> 00:16:44,010
After the initialization is done,

498
00:16:44,010 --> 00:16:45,030
we start running.

499
00:16:45,030 --> 00:16:45,970
So take a look, everyone.

500
00:16:45,970 --> 00:16:47,590
Suppose at the very beginning,

501
00:16:47,590 --> 00:16:50,790
it starts running from this task on the left

502
00:16:50,790 --> 00:16:52,670
so what does this represent

503
00:16:52,670 --> 00:16:53,850
this represents the pc value

504
00:16:53,850 --> 00:16:54,990
we talked about this earlier, right

505
00:16:54,990 --> 00:16:56,850
it's our program's

506
00:16:56,850 --> 00:16:58,910
current execution address

507
00:16:58,910 --> 00:17:00,370
this pc pointer

508
00:17:00,370 --> 00:17:01,790
the value in the pc register

509
00:17:01,790 --> 00:17:04,870
so first we run this task on the left

510
00:17:04,870 --> 00:17:07,670
first we execute the first i instruction

511
00:17:07,670 --> 00:17:09,870
then we execute the second i+1 instruction

512
00:17:09,870 --> 00:17:12,770
now the key is when it executes this instruction

513
00:17:12,770 --> 00:17:14,430
for example, this task on the left

514
00:17:14,430 --> 00:17:16,650
wants to voluntarily give up the CPU

515
00:17:16,650 --> 00:17:19,950
so it has to call this switch to function

516
00:17:19,950 --> 00:17:22,230
now everyone knows that this call function

517
00:17:22,230 --> 00:17:23,490
not call this function, but the call instruction

518
00:17:23,490 --> 00:17:24,850
this call instruction

519
00:17:24,850 --> 00:17:27,890
in assembly, what does it actually do

520
00:17:27,890 --> 00:17:30,210
it actually puts

521
00:17:30,210 --> 00:17:31,570
As we mentioned earlier,

522
00:17:31,570 --> 00:17:34,170
it will take the next instruction after this instruction,

523
00:17:34,170 --> 00:17:37,110
which is the address of this instruction, and put it where?

524
00:17:37,110 --> 00:17:40,630
It will put it into the CPU's RA register,

525
00:17:40,630 --> 00:17:40,950
right?

526
00:17:40,950 --> 00:17:43,450
We covered this earlier, right?

527
00:17:43,450 --> 00:17:46,070
So when we execute this call instruction,

528
00:17:46,070 --> 00:17:48,810
it will take the address of the RA + M instruction,

529
00:17:48,810 --> 00:17:51,530
and save it into RA.

530
00:17:51,530 --> 00:17:53,810
This is all just the normal function call process,

531
00:17:53,810 --> 00:17:54,390
right?

532
00:17:54,390 --> 00:17:56,010
And then,

533
00:17:56,010 --> 00:17:59,930
it will jump into the switch2 function to execute.

534
00:17:59,930 --> 00:18:02,290
Now, this switch2 function,

535
00:18:02,290 --> 00:18:04,630
for switching this upper context,

536
00:18:04,630 --> 00:18:06,470
is the core logic,

537
00:18:06,470 --> 00:18:07,950
its first step,

538
00:18:07,950 --> 00:18:09,370
is to save the previous one.

539
00:18:09,370 --> 00:18:10,290
The previous one, as we know,

540
00:18:10,290 --> 00:18:11,770
now what we mean by the previous one is that one.

541
00:18:11,770 --> 00:18:14,290
The previous, the previous context

542
00:18:14,290 --> 00:18:16,010
it becomes next instead

543
00:18:16,010 --> 00:18:18,230
because we are about to switch to it

544
00:18:18,230 --> 00:18:20,030
So when we say the previous context

545
00:18:20,030 --> 00:18:21,090
we actually mean it

546
00:18:21,090 --> 00:18:23,670
Now, its context,

547
00:18:23,670 --> 00:18:24,670
what we mean by context,

548
00:18:24,670 --> 00:18:25,670
is really just

549
00:18:25,670 --> 00:18:27,430
what we call saving

550
00:18:27,430 --> 00:18:30,310
the idea of saving the context,

551
00:18:30,310 --> 00:18:30,870
basically means that

552
00:18:30,870 --> 00:18:33,670
we take the values of these registers inside the CPU

553
00:18:33,670 --> 00:18:35,850
and move them out of the registers

554
00:18:35,850 --> 00:18:39,410
into the memory area corresponding to this task

555
00:18:39,410 --> 00:18:40,850
into that memory

556
00:18:40,850 --> 00:18:41,990
into that context area

557
00:18:41,990 --> 00:18:43,410
and save them there

558
00:18:43,410 --> 00:18:44,790
and save them there

559
00:18:44,790 --> 00:18:45,590
So as you can see,

560
00:18:45,590 --> 00:18:47,330
this value, originally this was RA plus M

561
00:18:47,330 --> 00:18:50,550
So in the following process,

562
00:18:50,550 --> 00:18:51,750
after saving it,

563
00:18:51,750 --> 00:18:53,430
then for the one on the left,

564
00:18:53,430 --> 00:18:55,530
go into its stack frame,

565
00:18:55,530 --> 00:18:57,150
and this ra will become what?

566
00:18:57,150 --> 00:18:57,710
ra plus m.

567
00:18:57,710 --> 00:18:58,410
Right?

568
00:18:58,410 --> 00:19:02,220
So once this becomes ra plus m,

569
00:19:02,220 --> 00:19:04,400
this is saved,

570
00:19:04,400 --> 00:19:05,680
and of course there are some other registers as well,

571
00:19:05,680 --> 00:19:06,400
